1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,160
Sam sat down on a Wednesday afternoon to turn the angle map into pixels, coffee in

2
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:08,210
hand, expecting this to be the easy part of the day.

3
00:00:08,210 --> 00:00:13,137
This works the same way regardless of which specific tool you're using, so I'll show

4
00:00:13,137 --> 00:00:17,302
the mechanics with one real example rather than a tour of every option.

5
00:00:17,302 --> 00:00:21,584
As of this recording, tools like Ideogram support batch generation from a

6
00:00:21,584 --> 00:00:26,453
spreadsheet: one row per prompt, up to hundreds at once, with in-image text handled

7
00:00:26,453 --> 00:00:26,980
directly.

8
00:00:26,980 --> 00:00:31,732
Twenty rows on the angle map becomes twenty rows on a prompt sheet becomes twenty

9
00:00:31,732 --> 00:00:32,905
images in one batch.

10
00:00:32,905 --> 00:00:37,773
Before this course's approach, Sam would have opened a single-image tool, typed one

11
00:00:37,773 --> 00:00:41,234
prompt, waited, and either kept the result or started over.

12
00:00:41,234 --> 00:00:45,985
Twenty variants meant twenty separate rounds of that, each one a fresh wait and a

13
00:00:45,985 --> 00:00:49,094
fresh chance to second-guess himself halfway through.

14
00:00:49,094 --> 00:00:54,021
A spreadsheet queue skips all of that: write every row once, submit the whole sheet,

15
00:00:54,021 --> 00:00:57,424
and walk away rather than babysitting one image at a time.

16
00:00:57,424 --> 00:01:00,591
Notice what didn't change between Section two and now.

17
00:01:00,591 --> 00:01:04,228
You're not inventing twenty new ideas at the generation stage.

18
00:01:04,228 --> 00:01:07,102
You're transcribing twenty ideas you already had.

19
00:01:07,102 --> 00:01:11,853
If you find yourself improvising a new angle while writing prompts, that's a sign

20
00:01:11,853 --> 00:01:15,666
the angle map wasn't finished, not a sign you're being efficient.

21
00:01:16,216 --> 00:01:18,769
Here's the actual translation, row by row.

22
00:01:18,769 --> 00:01:22,174
The angle map already told you the rung and the feeling.

23
00:01:22,174 --> 00:01:26,855
Turning that into a prompt is mostly transcription: what's in frame, what the

24
00:01:26,855 --> 00:01:30,199
overlay says, and which exact brand colour has to hold.

25
00:01:30,199 --> 00:01:35,123
A full prompt row usually holds four or five fields: what's in frame, the pose or

26
00:01:35,123 --> 00:01:40,230
setting, the exact overlay text, the brand colour, and a style note like lighting or

27
00:01:40,230 --> 00:01:40,534
crop.

28
00:01:40,534 --> 00:01:44,729
Fill those in once per row and the sheet does the repetition for you.

29
00:01:44,729 --> 00:01:49,775
This is where twenty rows earns its keep over four: the marginal cost of row twenty

30
00:01:49,775 --> 00:01:54,760
is the same few minutes of typing as row one, not another booking, another studio,

31
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:55,733
another invoice.

32
00:01:55,733 --> 00:02:00,536
Do this for all twenty rows, queue the batch, and in the time it takes to get a

33
00:02:00,536 --> 00:02:02,481
coffee, you have twenty statics.

34
00:02:02,481 --> 00:02:05,399
That part is genuinely as easy as it sounds now.

35
00:02:05,399 --> 00:02:08,135
What comes back is where it gets interesting.

36
00:02:08,135 --> 00:02:13,242
One detail worth locking in the prompt every time: the exact brand colour, stated as

37
00:02:13,242 --> 00:02:18,105
a hex value if the tool accepts one, not just "navy." "Navy" is an instruction a

38
00:02:18,105 --> 00:02:20,537
model can interpret several honest ways.

39
00:02:20,537 --> 00:02:21,632
A hex code is not.

40
00:02:21,632 --> 00:02:26,434
You'll see in a moment why that distinction matters more than it sounds like it

41
00:02:26,434 --> 00:02:26,860
should.

42
00:02:27,410 --> 00:02:32,520
Here's what actually came back, and it's worth sitting with the real numbers rather

43
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:33,751
than a tidy summary.

44
00:02:33,751 --> 00:02:36,336
All twenty generated on brief and on time.

45
00:02:36,336 --> 00:02:37,999
Most got the product right.

46
00:02:37,999 --> 00:02:43,108
But only thirteen of twenty held the exact Harlan Navy: several variants drifted to

47
00:02:43,108 --> 00:02:45,755
a lighter, more generic royal blue instead.

48
00:02:45,755 --> 00:02:50,742
And the spec overlay, the exact fifteen to twenty millimetres of mercury language

49
00:02:50,742 --> 00:02:55,851
from the product truth document, garbled on five of the twenty, exactly the kind of

50
00:02:55,851 --> 00:02:59,853
failure Section 3.1 told you to expect from longer on-image text.

51
00:02:59,853 --> 00:03:03,978
Notice these two failures didn't show up in the same seven statics.

52
00:03:03,978 --> 00:03:08,841
They're independent problems with independent causes, which matters, because it

53
00:03:08,841 --> 00:03:11,919
means fixing one won't accidentally fix the other.

54
00:03:11,919 --> 00:03:16,228
Each needs its own answer, and each gets one in the next two lectures.

55
00:03:16,228 --> 00:03:18,876
Worth naming why the colour drifted at all.

56
00:03:18,876 --> 00:03:23,800
A generator reads "navy" as a broad instruction, not a locked value, and it will

57
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:28,664
happily produce anything a reasonable person might call navy: sometimes exactly

58
00:03:28,664 --> 00:03:32,111
Harlan's shade, sometimes a brighter, more generic blue.

59
00:03:32,111 --> 00:03:34,266
That's not the tool malfunctioning.

60
00:03:34,266 --> 00:03:39,006
It's doing exactly what an unlocked instruction asked for, which is the whole

61
00:03:39,006 --> 00:03:44,054
argument for stating the colour as a hex value instead of a word, the fix the next

62
00:03:44,054 --> 00:03:45,963
lecture actually walks through.

63
00:03:46,513 --> 00:03:50,885
It's tempting to read "seven of twenty need fixes" as a failed batch.

64
00:03:50,885 --> 00:03:51,265
Is it?

65
00:03:51,265 --> 00:03:54,180
Look at the two columns again before deciding.

66
00:03:54,180 --> 00:03:56,778
Thirteen of twenty needed nothing at all.

67
00:03:56,778 --> 00:04:01,910
The seven that did fail, failed in two specific, named, entirely expected ways: a

68
00:04:01,910 --> 00:04:07,233
colour consistency problem and a long-text problem, both flagged in the last lecture

69
00:04:07,233 --> 00:04:09,197
before generation even started.

70
00:04:09,197 --> 00:04:13,189
Neither failure is mysterious, and neither is expensive to fix.

71
00:04:13,189 --> 00:04:18,512
That's a very different batch from one where you don't know why anything went wrong.

72
00:04:18,512 --> 00:04:23,264
Compare that to what a batch looks like when nobody read Section 3.1 first.

73
00:04:23,264 --> 00:04:28,523
Same failure rate, roughly, but every flawed static reads as a mystery instead of a

74
00:04:28,523 --> 00:04:33,656
checklist item, and the instinct becomes "maybe this tool just isn't good enough"

75
00:04:33,656 --> 00:04:38,725
rather than "fix the colour and the overlay." Knowing what to expect changes the

76
00:04:38,725 --> 00:04:43,857
entire emotional experience of getting these results back, not just the technical

77
00:04:43,857 --> 00:04:44,935
response to them.

78
00:04:44,935 --> 00:04:47,026
It also changes what you do next.

79
00:04:47,026 --> 00:04:51,968
Someone who reads this as a failed batch starts second-guessing the brief, the

80
00:04:51,968 --> 00:04:53,489
angle, even the product.

81
00:04:53,489 --> 00:04:58,748
Someone who reads it as an expected batch opens the next lecture and fixes a colour

82
00:04:58,748 --> 00:04:59,128
value.

83
00:04:59,128 --> 00:05:02,740
Same twenty statics, two completely different afternoons.

84
00:05:03,290 --> 00:05:06,987
One honest limit before you get excited about how fast this was.

85
00:05:06,987 --> 00:05:11,493
A batch with seven known problems in it is not ready for Section seven's test.

86
00:05:11,493 --> 00:05:16,173
Running it anyway means testing seven ads that fail for a reason you already know

87
00:05:16,173 --> 00:05:20,968
about, which wastes real budget confirming something you didn't need a test to tell

88
00:05:20,968 --> 00:05:21,199
you.

89
00:05:21,199 --> 00:05:25,878
The next two lectures fix exactly these two failures, properly, so the batch that

90
00:05:25,878 --> 00:05:29,864
actually launches is twenty for twenty on the things you can control.

91
00:05:29,864 --> 00:05:31,944
Put a number on that waste, roughly.

92
00:05:31,944 --> 00:05:36,565
If seven of twenty statics are already known to be flawed and they run in a test

93
00:05:36,565 --> 00:05:39,974
anyway, you're not learning whether Harlan's message works.

94
00:05:39,974 --> 00:05:44,653
You're re-discovering, at real media spend, a fact the checklist already told you

95
00:05:44,653 --> 00:05:45,173
for free.

96
00:05:45,173 --> 00:05:49,910
That's the expensive way to learn something you already knew, and it's exactly the

97
00:05:49,910 --> 00:05:52,799
mistake this checklist exists to make unnecessary.

98
00:05:53,349 --> 00:05:58,247
Next lecture takes on the colour problem directly, and it's a smaller fix than the

99
00:05:58,247 --> 00:05:59,859
failure rate makes it look.

100
00:05:59,859 --> 00:06:02,786
Before that, do Harlan's count on your own batch.

101
00:06:02,786 --> 00:06:07,684
Don't fix anything yet, just check: how many statics hold your exact brand colour,

102
00:06:07,684 --> 00:06:10,910
and how many keep every word of on-image text legible.

103
00:06:10,910 --> 00:06:15,808
Thirteen of twenty is roughly where Harlan started, and it's a fine bar to compare

104
00:06:15,808 --> 00:06:19,093
yourself against, not a score to beat on the first try.

105
00:06:19,093 --> 00:06:21,661
Write both numbers down next to your batch.

106
00:06:21,661 --> 00:06:26,260
They become artifact B03's actual before picture: the flawed batch a fix gets

107
00:06:26,260 --> 00:06:30,501
measured against, rather than a vague sense that some of it needs work.

108
00:06:30,501 --> 00:06:35,340
Knowing your own two numbers now is what makes Section 3.3's fix land immediately

109
00:06:35,340 --> 00:06:37,012
instead of staying abstract.
